\title{Rota Arrangements in Dimensions One and Two:\\A Complete Exchange Analysis}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 29, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Rota's basis conjecture asks whether $n$ prescribed bases of an $n$-dimensional vector space can be independently reordered and placed as the rows of an $n\times n$ array so that every column is also a basis. The unrestricted conjecture remains open. This paper gives a self-contained treatment of dimensions one and two over an arbitrary division ring. We define a Rota arrangement by row-permutation and column-independence conditions, prove that each independent column yields a basis by dimension counting, and establish the rank-one case. The main ingredient in rank two is a two-by-two exchange lemma: for independent pairs $(a,b)$ and $(c,d)$, either the parallel crossing $(a,c),(b,d)$ consists of two independent pairs or the alternative crossing $(a,d),(b,c)$ does. This dichotomy yields a constructive rank-two theorem and a constant-time algorithm. We discuss determinant implementations over fields, exact arithmetic examples, invariance and cyclic constructions, finite-field search, direct-sum questions, and the obstruction to extending a local exchange argument directly to higher rank.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Let $K$ be a division ring and let $V$ be a left $K$-vector space of finite dimension $n$. Suppose that $n$ ordered bases are given:
\[
B_i=(b_{i,1},\ldots,b_{i,n}),\qquad i=1,\ldots,n.
\]
Rota's basis conjecture asserts that there exist permutations $\pi_1,\ldots,\pi_n$ of $\{1,\ldots,n\}$ such that, for each column index $j$, the transversal family
\[
(b_{1,\pi_1(j)},b_{2,\pi_2(j)},\ldots,b_{n,\pi_n(j)})
\]
is a basis of $V$. Equivalently, one may reorder each input basis independently and use the reordered lists as rows of a square array in which all columns are bases.

The statement combines linear algebra with a global matching problem. Every row is already independent and spanning; the difficulty is to coordinate row permutations so that all transversals inherit these properties. The conjecture is unresolved in unrestricted rank, so low-dimensional cases are valuable both as boundary cases and as guides to the structure a general proof would need.

This paper isolates and proves the complete rank-one and rank-two theory. The rank-one case is immediate. Rank two is governed by a precise exchange dichotomy involving four vectors and the two possible perfect matchings between the two input bases. Although the geometric intuition is elementary over $\mathbb R$, the proof is stated over an arbitrary division ring and relies only on linear independence and division by nonzero scalars.

The contributions are a minimal definition of a Rota arrangement, a dimension argument promoting independent columns to bases, complete proofs in ranks one and two, a two-by-two exchange theorem, and exact constructive algorithms. No result assumes commutativity of scalar multiplication. Determinants are used only in the computational specialization to commutative fields.

\section{Algebraic preliminaries}

A \emph{division ring} $K$ is a ring in which every nonzero element has a multiplicative inverse; multiplication need not commute. A \emph{left $K$-vector space} is an abelian group $V$ equipped with a left scalar action satisfying the usual laws.

A finite indexed family $(v_i)_{i\in I}$ is \emph{linearly independent} when
\[
\sum_{i\in I}\lambda_i v_i=0
\]
implies $\lambda_i=0$ for every $i$. A \emph{basis} is a linearly independent family spanning $V$. In dimension $n$, every linearly independent family of exactly $n$ vectors is a basis. For a pair, independence says that $sx+ty=0$ forces $s=t=0$; in particular, neither member of an independent pair is zero.

\begin{lemma}[Proportionality of a dependent nonzero pair]
Let $x,y\in V$, with $x$ and $y$ nonzero. If $(x,y)$ is linearly dependent, then the vectors are related by a nonzero scalar multiple.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Dependence supplies $s,t$, not both zero, such that $sx+ty=0$. If $t\ne0$, multiplication on the left by $t^{-1}$ gives
\[
y=(-t^{-1}s)x.
\]
The multiplier cannot vanish because $y\ne0$. If $t=0$, then $s\ne0$, and division by $s$ would force $x=0$, a contradiction. Reversing the vectors gives the opposite orientation when needed.
\end{proof}

This lemma replaces the geometric statement that dependent nonzero vectors lie on the same line through the origin.

\section{Rota arrangements}

\begin{definition}[Rota arrangement]
Let $B_1,\ldots,B_n$ be ordered bases of an $n$-dimensional left vector space $V$. A \emph{Rota arrangement} is an array $G=(g_{ij})_{1\le i,j\le n}$ such that:
\begin{enumerate}
\item for every $i$, there is a permutation $\pi_i$ satisfying $g_{ij}=b_{i,\pi_i(j)}$ for all $j$;
\item for every $j$, the family $(g_{1j},\ldots,g_{nj})$ is linearly independent.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Column promotion]
Every column of a Rota arrangement is a basis of $V$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
A column consists of $n$ vectors and is linearly independent by definition. Since $V$ has dimension $n$, it spans $V$ and is a basis.
\end{proof}

The row condition also ensures that each row remains a basis, since permutations preserve independence and span. Thus this formulation is equivalent to requiring bases in both directions.

\section{Rank one}

\begin{theorem}[Rank-One Rota Basis Theorem]
Let $V$ be a one-dimensional left vector space over a division ring $K$, and let $B_1=(b)$ be a basis. Then the $1\times1$ array $(b)$ is a Rota arrangement.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The only row is the supplied basis under the identity permutation. A basis vector is nonzero. If $sb=0$, division by a nonzero $s$ would imply $b=0$, so necessarily $s=0$. The unique column is independent and hence a basis.
\end{proof}

\section{The two-by-two exchange lemma}

Let the two input bases be $(a,b)$ and $(c,d)$. Once the first row is fixed, only two relative arrangements remain:
\[
G_{\parallel}=\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{pmatrix},
\qquad
G_{\times}=\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\d&c\end{pmatrix}.
\]
Their columns are respectively $(a,c),(b,d)$ and $(a,d),(b,c)$.

\begin{theorem}[Two-by-Two Exchange Lemma]
Let $a,b,c,d\in V$. Suppose $(a,b)$ and $(c,d)$ are linearly independent. Then either both $(a,c)$ and $(b,d)$ are linearly independent, or both $(a,d)$ and $(b,c)$ are linearly independent.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $(a,c)$ and $(b,d)$ are both independent, the first alternative holds. Suppose otherwise.

First consider the case that $(a,c)$ is dependent. All four vectors are nonzero because they belong to independent input pairs. The proportionality lemma says that $a$ and $c$ are nonzero scalar multiples. If $(a,d)$ were dependent, then $d$ would be proportional to $a$ and hence to $c$, contradicting independence of $(c,d)$. Thus $(a,d)$ is independent. If $(b,c)$ were dependent, then $b$ would be proportional to $c$ and hence to $a$, contradicting independence of $(a,b)$. Thus $(b,c)$ is independent.

It remains to consider the case in which $(a,c)$ is independent but $(b,d)$ is dependent. Then $b$ and $d$ are nonzero scalar multiples. Dependence of $(a,d)$ would make $a$ proportional to $d$ and hence to $b$, contradicting independence of $(a,b)$. Dependence of $(b,c)$ would make $c$ proportional to $b$ and hence to $d$, contradicting independence of $(c,d)$. Therefore $(a,d)$ and $(b,c)$ are both independent.
\end{proof}

The alternatives may both hold. For four generic directions in the real plane, all four cross-pairs are independent.

\subsection{Coefficient-level interpretation}

Suppose $(b,d)$ is dependent. There are $s,t$, not both zero, with
\[
sb+td=0.
\]
The nonzero status of $b,d$ forces $t\ne0$ after orienting the equation, and
\[
d=(-t^{-1}s)b,
\]
with nonzero multiplier. A relation $ua+vd=0$ becomes
\[
ua+v(-t^{-1}s)b=0.
\]
Independence of $(a,b)$ forces $u=0$ and then $v=0$. This proves independence of $(a,d)$. The corresponding substitution against $(c,d)$ proves independence of $(b,c)$. Scalar products remain in order, explaining validity over noncommutative division rings.

\subsection{Geometric and matching interpretations}

Over $\mathbb R$, write $[x]$ for the line generated by a nonzero vector $x$. Independence of a pair means inequality of directions. The hypotheses say $[a]\ne[b]$ and $[c]\ne[d]$. If $[a]\ne[c]$ and $[b]\ne[d]$, the parallel pairing works. If $[a]=[c]$, then $[a]\ne[d]$ and $[b]\ne[c]$, so the crossed pairing works; the case $[b]=[d]$ is symmetric.

Equivalently, build a bipartite graph with left vertices $a,b$, right vertices $c,d$, and edges joining independent pairs. A valid arrangement is a perfect matching. The theorem states that the input basis conditions force one of the graph's two possible perfect matchings to exist.

\section{The rank-two theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Rank-Two Rota Basis Theorem]
Let $V$ be a two-dimensional left vector space over a division ring $K$. For every two ordered bases $(a,b)$ and $(c,d)$ of $V$, there is a $2\times2$ Rota arrangement.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply the Two-by-Two Exchange Lemma. If $(a,c)$ and $(b,d)$ are independent, choose
\[
G=\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{pmatrix}.
\]
If not, the alternative pairs $(a,d)$ and $(b,c)$ are independent, and choose
\[
G=\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\d&c\end{pmatrix}.
\]
In both cases the rows are permutations of the input bases and the columns are independent. Column promotion makes them bases.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[One-row normalization]
In rank two, the first input basis may remain in its original order; at most the second basis must be swapped.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Both constructions in the theorem retain $(a,b)$ as the first row.
\end{proof}

\section{Constructive algorithms}

\begin{algorithm}[Abstract rank-two arrangement]
Given bases $(a,b)$ and $(c,d)$ and an independence test:
\begin{enumerate}
\item test whether $(a,c)$ is independent;
\item test whether $(b,d)$ is independent;
\item if both tests succeed, output rows $(a,b)$ and $(c,d)$;
\item otherwise output rows $(a,b)$ and $(d,c)$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{algorithm}

Correctness follows directly from the exchange lemma. The method uses two independence tests for selection and constant auxiliary storage. A defensive implementation can test both output columns as well.

Over a commutative field, for $x=(x_1,x_2)$ and $y=(y_1,y_2)$ define
\[
\Delta(x,y)=x_1y_2-x_2y_1.
\]
Then $(x,y)$ is independent exactly when $\Delta(x,y)\ne0$. For exact rational or integer input, the check is exact. For floating-point data, a threshold should be scaled by vector norms. In fixed dimension each determinant takes two multiplications and one subtraction, so total arithmetic complexity and memory use are $O(1)$.

An exhaustive audit may instead enumerate the identity and swap permutations for the second row and record both column determinants in each case. The theorem guarantees that at least one candidate succeeds whenever both input rows are bases.

\section{Numerical examples}

\subsection{Straight pairing succeeds}
Let
\[
a=(1,0),\quad b=(0,1),\quad c=(1,1),\quad d=(2,1).
\]
The row determinants are $1$ and $-1$. The straight column determinants are
\[
\Delta(a,c)=1,\qquad \Delta(b,d)=-2.
\]
Thus the unswapped grid is a Rota arrangement.

\subsection{A forced swap}
Let
\[
a=(1,0),\quad b=(0,1),\quad c=(2,0),\quad d=(1,3).
\]
The row determinants are $1$ and $6$, but $\Delta(a,c)=0$, so the straight arrangement fails. The alternative determinants are
\[
\Delta(a,d)=3,\qquad \Delta(b,c)=-2.
\]
Swapping the second row succeeds.

\subsection{Both pairings succeed}
Let
\[
a=(1,0),\quad b=(0,1),\quad c=(1,1),\quad d=(1,-1).
\]
The four cross determinants are $1,-1,-1,-1$, all nonzero. Both arrangements work, illustrating that the dichotomy guarantees existence rather than uniqueness.

\section{Structural observations and applications}

The rank-two theorem models scheduling with preservation of information. Suppose each of two sensor packages contains two measurements sufficient to reconstruct a two-parameter state. The theorem schedules one measurement from each package at each of two times while keeping each time slot sufficient for reconstruction. The same structure appears in transversal experimental designs, robust feature assignment, and coordinate encodings.

A structured construction exists in every positive dimension when all input rows are copies of one cyclically indexed basis. Index the basis by $\mathbb Z/n\mathbb Z$ and define
\[
g_{ij}=b_{i+j}.
\]
For fixed $i$, translation $j\mapsto i+j$ is a permutation, so each row is a copy of the basis. For fixed $j$, translation $i\mapsto i+j$ is also a permutation, so each column is a basis. This provides a broad family of explicit Latin-square arrangements, though it does not address arbitrary input bases.

Existence is also invariant under independently relabeling each supplied basis. Any arrangement for a relabeled family may be composed with the inverse relabelings, and conversely. This symmetry justifies fixing one row order in rank two and can substantially reduce computational search in higher ranks.

\section{Limits of the rank-two method}

The proof depends on a feature special to pairs: a dependent pair of nonzero vectors occupies one proportionality class. For three vectors, dependence can occur without any two being proportional. The vectors $x,y,x+y$ are the elementary example. Therefore a graph recording pairwise independent choices does not control rank-three columns.

Higher-rank work must track multi-vector dependence through matroid rank, nonvanishing minors, circuits, or exterior products. The search also expands: for rank three there are $(3!)^3=216$ raw row-permutation choices before symmetry reduction. Repairing one dependent column may create dependence elsewhere, so the one-swap argument does not directly generalize.

Nevertheless, the small case suggests a strategy: identify local obstructions, show that each obstruction forces compatibility elsewhere, and organize those implications into a global exchange procedure.

\section{Future research directions}

Five concrete extensions arise.
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Rank-three arrangements.} Prove that every three bases of a three-dimensional vector space over a division ring admit a Rota arrangement.
\item \textbf{Constant-family cyclic arrangements.} Establish the cyclic construction uniformly for every positive $n$ with indices in $\mathbb Z/n\mathbb Z$.
\item \textbf{Independent row-reindexing invariance.} Prove equivalence of arrangement existence before and after arbitrary independent input-row permutations.
\item \textbf{Direct-sum closure under block-compatible input.} Determine hypotheses under which arrangements of sizes $m$ and $n$ combine into one of size $m+n$ in a direct sum, with all rows respecting a common block decomposition.
\item \textbf{Finite-field exhaustive rank three.} In $\mathbb F_2^3$, enumerate triples of bases modulo natural symmetries and either construct an arrangement for every triple or extract a counterexample.
\end{enumerate}

The finite-field program is especially concrete. The space $\mathbb F_2^3$ has seven nonzero vectors. Every basis is an ordered triple with nonzero determinant. General linear coordinate changes can fix the first basis, while row and column symmetries reduce duplicates. Even though such a computation would not settle arbitrary rank three, it could reveal exchange patterns supporting a conceptual argument.

\section{Conclusion}

A Rota arrangement turns $n$ given row bases into $n$ simultaneous column bases by independently permuting row entries. In rank one, the identity arrangement suffices. In rank two, the complete problem reduces to two possible cross-pairings of four vectors.

The Two-by-Two Exchange Lemma proves that two independent input pairs cannot make both crossings fail: either $(a,c)$ with $(b,d)$ works, or $(a,d)$ with $(b,c)$ works. This yields a constructive theorem over arbitrary division rings and a constant-time determinant algorithm over ordinary fields.

The rank-two case converts local dependence into forced independence in the complementary pairing. Higher rank requires control of genuine multi-vector dependencies, but the small case supplies a clean definition, complete base theory, computational tests, and a precise model of a successful exchange argument.

\end{document}

