<details><summary>Click to reveal precise definitions</summary>

A Rota arrangement for $n$ bases of an $n$-dimensional vector space is an $n\times n$ grid in which each row is a permutation of its assigned basis and each column is linearly independent. Every column then contains $n$ independent vectors in dimension $n$, so it is automatically a basis.

</details>

## A cyclic family in every dimension

When all input rows are copies of one basis, modular addition builds the grid immediately.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal why the cyclic construction works</summary>

Index a basis by $\mathbb Z/n\mathbb Z$ and put $g_{ij}=b_{i+j}$. For fixed $i$, translation by $i$ permutes all basis labels, so row $i$ is a basis. For fixed $j$, translation by $j$ does the same for a column. Hence every horizontal and vertical line is a permutation of the same basis.

</details>

## Looking toward rank three

A general-purpose finite search provides an experimental bridge to the next case:

{{algorithm:2}}

Unlike rank two, pairwise independence no longer ensures independence of a whole column. Readers may consult introductions to [matroid theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matroid) and [linear independence](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear_independence) for the broader exchange framework. A finite search over the two-element field is a concrete next experiment, while a conceptual rank-three proof remains a central target.

